    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d451">

                <pb facs="0795" n="663" xml:id="pg663"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">868.00/7–2745</note>
                <head>No. 451<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Greece
                    </hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >MacVeagh</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to Prime Minister
                            Voulgaris
                    </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d451-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Athens</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-07-13">July 13, 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: Under instructions from my
                    Government I have the honor to inform Your Excellency that in view of Allied
                    obligations to Greece and the undertakings
                    at Yalta, as well as of Article IX of the Varkiza Agreement, the Goverments of
                    the United States of America and the United Kingdom of Great Britain and
                    Northern Ireland have agreed and are willing, in the event of such action being
                    agreeable to the Royal Hellenic Government, to approach the Government of the
                    Union of Soviet Socialist Republics and the Government of France with the
                    proposal that the approaching Greek national elections be held under the
                    supervision of the four Allies named.</p>
                <p>My Government feels that if the Royal Hellenic Government accepts this invitation
                    in principle, it should be possible to take up the matter with the Soviet
                    Government during the immediately forthcoming international Conference at
                    Potsdam, while the French Government could be approached at the same time by the
                    United States and United Kingdom Ambassadors in Paris. I am therefore instructed
                    to ask that the Royal Hellenic Government give it the earliest possible
                    consideration.</p>
                <p>In anticipation of Your Excellency’s reply, I have [etc.]<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d451-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lincoln MacVeagh</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d451-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d451-f1-content">
                <p>Enclosure 1 to MacVeagh’s despatch No. 1349
                    to the Secretary of State, July 27, 1945. MacVeagh sent a summary of this communication and the text of
                        Voulgaris’ endorsement (see footnote
                    2, <hi rendition="#italic">infra</hi>) to the Acting Secretary of State in
                    telegram No. 704 of July 13 (file No. 868.00/7–1345).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d451-f2" n="2" xml:id="d451-f2-content">
                <p>
                    MacVeagh presented this letter to
                        Voulgaris on the morning of July 13.
                    The latter returned it at once with the following manuscript endorsement: “On
                    behalf of the Royal Hellenic Government I agree[.] Athens, the 13th July, 1945
                    The Prime Minister Admiral P.
                    Voulgaris”.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
